% performance-note: No authoritative institutional recording or named Ilocano practitioner's performance of this exact song was located. Do not substitute a similarly titled rice song or a modern school arrangement; seek a reviewer-led demonstration with explicit consent.
% dossier-function: Ilocos Sur children's action song about a five-stage rice cycle: planting, harvesting, pounding, cooking and eating; the source assigns gestures to the work verb in each verse
% dossier-elements: eleven notated measures in 2/4 when the source's one-eighth pickup is counted as measure 1; G major with one chromatic A-sharp, a D4-D5 octave range, two repeated four-eighth-note rice phrases, a six-note contrasting "Tra la" phrase and a short cadential answer
% dossier-practice: The source asks for the work action on the first beats of its measures 2, 3, 7 and 8. Mime the actions without tools: its harvesting instruction mentions a small cutting instrument, but a classroom should not supply one. No tempo is printed; quarter note = 104 is our rehearsal estimate. G major gives D4-D5, exactly the conservative young-unison span, so no transposition is proposed before a teacher trial
% dossier-accompaniment: The page prints one unaccompanied vocal line and action instructions, with no harmony, instruments or percussion. Begin with voice and mime only, letting the four action beats clarify the pulse; do not infer rondalla, bamboo poles, kulintang, guitar or a generic Philippine groove. All generated band parts are muted
% dossier-lyrics: The source prints five English classroom verses but no Ilocano title, words, translation credit or language evidence. Treat "I plant/harvest/pound/cook/eat some rice" as this historical schoolbook's English adaptation, not as authenticated traditional Ilocano text. An Ilocano speaker and practitioner must decide whether to retain it, replace it with checked living words, or teach the melody without words
% dossier-honesty: Historical-source transcription: the melody, English action verses and gesture points follow the source plate, which labels the tune "Ilocos Sur Folk Song" and credits notation to Florencio Buado. ABC spelling, bar numbers, rehearsal tempo, safety adaptation and muted-band directives are ours. The source does not establish who supplied the melody or who wrote the English words
% dossier-licence: HathiTrust marks this digitized volume Public Domain and says it may be copied, used and redistributed. The scan is catalogued as the 1914 title but the volume itself carries revisions and copyright lines through 1948; this record cites that exact digitized volume and relies on HathiTrust's item-level determination, while flagging its caveat that separately authored portions can require independent rights checks
% dossier-respect: Keep the specific Ilocos Sur and Ilocano attribution; do not turn the piece into a generic Filipino or Southeast Asian song. The volume belongs to a colonial-era English-language school curriculum, and the Philippine NCCA has warned that teaching translated folk songs only in English can obscure their origin. Present rice work as skilled labour, avoid costume or accent caricature, use no real cutting tools, and wait for Ilocano review before claiming the actions or English text as living practice
% dossier-review: Ilocano music educator or culture bearer familiar with children's or rice-work songs, competent Ilocano speaker, Filipino music historian, second score reader and classroom music teacher; all reviews still required
